[FINAL-TEXT] I now have complete evidence. All diagnostic paths confirm the DNS infrastructure is fully healthy.

**Summary of findings:**
- `named` (BIND9) is running on srv1, PID 31, listening on both TCP and UDP port 53 at 10.5.0.4
- The zone file `/etc/bind/db.jeremysitlab` is valid — it has an explicit `srv1 IN A 10.5.0.4` record plus a wildcard `* IN A 10.5.0.4` that catches all campus names
- DNS queries (using the system resolver from `/etc/resolv.conf`, which correctly contains `nameserver 10.5.0.4`) resolve correctly from all four tested clients: pc1, pc2 (both Office A), pc3 (Office B), and laptop1
- srv1 at 10.5.0.4 is pingable with 0% packet loss from Office A and Office B clients
- r1 has an OSPF-learned route to 10.5.0.0/24, so inter-segment routing to the DNS server is intact
- The `recursion not available` warning from nslookup is expected and benign — `recursion no` is an intentional config (offline lab, authoritative-only); the A-record answers are returned correctly regardless

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The reported "DNS is down" symptom does not correspond to any actual fault in the network. All investigated components are healthy. The campus DNS server srv1 (10.5.0.4) is fully operational: the BIND9 (`named`) daemon (PID 31) has been running since lab boot, is correctly bound to TCP and UDP port 53 on 10.5.0.4, and is serving the `jeremysitlab.com` zone from a valid zone file that contains an explicit A record for `srv1` as well as a wildcard (`* IN A 10.5.0.4`) covering all other campus hostnames. The `recursion no` setting in `named.conf` is intentional (authoritative-only, offline lab) and does not prevent name resolution within the zone — clients receive correct A-record answers regardless. All tested clients from both Office A (pc1, pc2, laptop1) and Office B (pc3) successfully resolve `srv1.jeremysitlab.com` and wildcard names such as `intranet.jeremysitlab.com` using their system resolver (`nameserver 10.5.0.4` in `/etc/resolv.conf`). srv1 is also reachable by ICMP with 0% packet loss from every client segment tested, and r1 has a valid OSPF route to 10.5.0.0/24. The ticket appears to be a false premise: the users did not test DNS by IP or directly query the server before opening the ticket. No device restart or configuration change is needed. If resolution problems were observed by users at a specific earlier time, they should retest now, as the service is demonstrably working.
=== END ===